1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:02,960
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:07,859
I'm Gite, and I am the founder and CEO
of Gigapype, and today I'm going to

3
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,629
tell you the story of why we built it.

4
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:13,009
To do that, I need to take you
somewhere first, a place I believe

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:17,072
most of you have probably already
been So let's picture this.

6
00:00:17,072 --> 00:00:20,991
It's 3:00 AM, it's your on-call
shift, and your phone starts ringing.

7
00:00:21,082 --> 00:00:21,692
You have a P1.

8
00:00:22,372 --> 00:00:26,411
Latency is up, error rates are
spiking, users are being impacted.

9
00:00:26,791 --> 00:00:30,821
So you get up, you go to your laptop,
and you open your metrics tool.

10
00:00:31,181 --> 00:00:32,171
The spike is right there.

11
00:00:32,631 --> 00:00:35,961
CPU climbing, request latency
up, something is wrong.

12
00:00:36,351 --> 00:00:37,631
But why is this happening?

13
00:00:37,641 --> 00:00:39,041
Metrics don't tell you why.

14
00:00:39,161 --> 00:00:41,941
So you switch and you open your logs tool.

15
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:44,851
You start filtering and you
find something, a flood of

16
00:00:45,101 --> 00:00:46,361
errors from one service.

17
00:00:46,401 --> 00:00:49,031
You made progress, but
which request caused it?

18
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:50,191
You need a trace.

19
00:00:50,411 --> 00:00:53,441
So you switch again and
you open your tracing tool.

20
00:00:53,891 --> 00:00:56,901
You try to find a trace, but you
struggle at first because the

21
00:00:56,901 --> 00:01:01,001
timestamps between logs, metrics,
and traces do not quite line up.

22
00:01:01,031 --> 00:01:04,781
So you need to adjust manually,
try to make it match between

23
00:01:04,821 --> 00:01:06,821
tools, and finally you find it.

24
00:01:07,531 --> 00:01:10,711
At this point, you are
15 minutes in at best.

25
00:01:11,121 --> 00:01:14,321
You've just figured out what the
problem is, but you still need to go

26
00:01:14,321 --> 00:01:17,751
to the code and find a place where
you need to tweak it and fix it.

27
00:01:18,461 --> 00:01:22,211
I bet most people in this room
have been in this exact place.

28
00:01:22,562 --> 00:01:26,171
Maybe not this incident line
up, but the feeling is the same.

29
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:33,621
And that crappy feeling is the basic
on-call experience nowadays, and it is

30
00:01:33,651 --> 00:01:36,351
caused by two flavors of the same problem.

31
00:01:36,882 --> 00:01:40,982
First, you are spread across
completely separated prope- products.

32
00:01:41,331 --> 00:01:43,232
Maybe you have Datadog for metrics.

33
00:01:43,232 --> 00:01:46,251
Maybe you have Kibana for
logs, Jaeger for traces.

34
00:01:46,621 --> 00:01:50,791
Those are different products, different
URLs, different query languages,

35
00:01:50,831 --> 00:01:55,502
different mental modules, and those were
never designed to talk to each other.

36
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:59,911
So what happens is that you need to
correlate your telemetry manually

37
00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:02,021
under pressure inside your head.

38
00:02:03,041 --> 00:02:04,241
The second one is sneakier.

39
00:02:04,691 --> 00:02:07,821
Maybe there are s- there are some
people here that believe they

40
00:02:07,821 --> 00:02:09,401
already solved this problem, right?

41
00:02:09,401 --> 00:02:11,611
Because they have
everything inside Grafana.

42
00:02:12,041 --> 00:02:14,571
So you have one place to
go and troubleshoot it all.

43
00:02:14,591 --> 00:02:16,041
You have only one UI.

44
00:02:17,022 --> 00:02:22,262
That's not entirely true because if you
go and investigate with a closer look,

45
00:02:22,531 --> 00:02:26,921
you can go to your data sources config,
and you will find that for each telemetry

46
00:02:26,981 --> 00:02:29,582
signal, you have a f- different entity.

47
00:02:29,852 --> 00:02:33,072
So in fact, you have four
different backends, four

48
00:02:33,111 --> 00:02:35,011
independent storage systems.

49
00:02:35,891 --> 00:02:39,271
This is the reason why the log panel
doesn't know about the metrics spike.

50
00:02:39,531 --> 00:02:43,362
Grafana doesn't automatically pull
the trace ID from the log line.

51
00:02:43,682 --> 00:02:47,351
So you are still jumping just
between one browser tab instead

52
00:02:47,352 --> 00:02:50,941
of four different ones, but you
are still the one responsible to

53
00:02:50,941 --> 00:02:52,701
correlate everything manually.

54
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:55,041
So one of these looks unified.

55
00:02:55,231 --> 00:02:56,181
Neither is.

56
00:02:56,291 --> 00:02:59,521
Both cost you the same 15
minutes to correlate signals.

57
00:02:59,882 --> 00:03:03,381
And behind both of them, there's a
structural reason for this to exist.

58
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,650
And that reason is mainstream
observability solutions make you

59
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:11,150
choose what matters most to you,
as if a well-rounded solution with

60
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:14,859
complete coverage at a reasonable
price was so much to ask for.

61
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:20,419
So at the end of the day, we are all
facing the triangle of trade-offs because

62
00:03:20,459 --> 00:03:24,630
let's face it, the best solution in terms
of observability is something that gives

63
00:03:24,630 --> 00:03:27,159
you the three points at the same time.

64
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:32,560
First, complete observability, logs,
metrics, traces, and profiles on the

65
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:35,029
same single backend in a single place.

66
00:03:35,529 --> 00:03:38,519
At the same time, it needs to be
affordable enough so it doesn't

67
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:39,849
get blocked by the management.

68
00:03:40,430 --> 00:03:44,189
And lastly, it should be operationally
simple so your team can focus on

69
00:03:44,189 --> 00:03:48,650
your services and products and not
on operating the observability stack.

70
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:53,620
This seems utopian because every time
that you try to get the three of them at

71
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:56,229
once, you face a triangle of trade-offs.

72
00:03:56,239 --> 00:04:00,289
For example, when you try to reach
for complete coverage and for it to

73
00:04:00,299 --> 00:04:04,489
be cheap, then you must run everything
yourself and this means that besides

74
00:04:04,489 --> 00:04:08,589
your actual job, now you have a part-time
one as an observability engineer.

75
00:04:09,719 --> 00:04:13,050
When you try to reach for complete
coverage and simplicity, you need

76
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:17,519
to go to a vendor and your bill
will be, let's say, significant.

77
00:04:18,139 --> 00:04:22,210
And if you want it to be cheap and
simple, you start cutting signals.

78
00:04:22,249 --> 00:04:24,959
You turn off the tracing
for non-critical services.

79
00:04:25,289 --> 00:04:27,469
You increase your metric scrape interval.

80
00:04:27,689 --> 00:04:31,029
You reduce the log retention
just to give you a few examples.

81
00:04:31,429 --> 00:04:36,429
And then for the one incident that needed
this data it doesn't get a proper RCA

82
00:04:36,449 --> 00:04:38,259
because there's no data to work with.

83
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:39,539
You are flying blind.

84
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:45,440
Every SRE team that I've talked to and
teams that I worked on have made one

85
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:47,519
of these compromises and we did too.

86
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:52,779
But this wouldn't be a talk about
observability without talking about the

87
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:58,309
cloud bill, the line item on the budget
that says observability infrastructure.

88
00:04:58,499 --> 00:04:59,990
But that's just the tip of the iceberg.

89
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:04,519
The part that doesn't show on any budget
is your time, the engineer's time.

90
00:05:05,130 --> 00:05:08,679
Every hour that you spend upgrading Loki
because it broke on a new Kubernetes

91
00:05:08,679 --> 00:05:14,010
version, every 15 minutes that you need to
invest to correlate your signals manually,

92
00:05:14,509 --> 00:05:18,569
every onboarding session when there is a
new teammate and you need to teach them

93
00:05:18,859 --> 00:05:23,459
the new different tools that they need to
care for the observability, the different

94
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:25,770
query languages, how to maintain it all.

95
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:30,969
And as if it was not already a nightmare,
it's about to get worse because

96
00:05:31,389 --> 00:05:36,089
with these new AI era, AI workloads
generate orders of magnitude more

97
00:05:36,089 --> 00:05:38,140
telemetry than anything before them.

98
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:42,560
So the, this per event pricing
module, this complex set of backends

99
00:05:43,230 --> 00:05:45,580
doesn't survive the AI era at all.

100
00:05:46,029 --> 00:05:50,379
And every one of these problems compounds
harder as your data volume grows.

101
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:54,749
So the more business that you
get, the more pain you experience

102
00:05:54,749 --> 00:05:56,129
with observability tools.

103
00:05:57,895 --> 00:06:01,925
At some point, we needed to stop
treating these limitations as the

104
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:04,505
standard of doing observability.

105
00:06:04,775 --> 00:06:09,275
It's not the way that it should
be, and we started do-doing

106
00:06:09,375 --> 00:06:10,785
or making different questions.

107
00:06:11,465 --> 00:06:14,555
What if these four
signals weren't separated?

108
00:06:14,725 --> 00:06:18,945
Not just displayed together in one
existing tool, but actually stored

109
00:06:18,945 --> 00:06:22,655
together, ingested together, queryable
together, natively correlated.

110
00:06:23,245 --> 00:06:27,155
What if for you to achieve this, you
didn't change or you didn't have to

111
00:06:27,155 --> 00:06:31,375
change your Prometheus exporters,
Loki agents, OpenTelemetry collectors?

112
00:06:31,855 --> 00:06:35,205
What if your dashboard just worked
without new plugins, without

113
00:06:35,305 --> 00:06:36,955
re-detecting the whole solution?

114
00:06:37,505 --> 00:06:40,895
So we asked these questions long
enough that we had to build the

115
00:06:40,895 --> 00:06:44,735
answer, and we built it because
we got tired of maintaining these

116
00:06:44,735 --> 00:06:49,155
multiplexed of backends, tired of
this cost to correlate our telemetry,

117
00:06:49,455 --> 00:06:51,625
and tired of the trade-off triangle.

118
00:06:51,965 --> 00:06:53,585
So we built Gigapipe.

119
00:06:54,675 --> 00:06:56,155
And what's Gigapipe?

120
00:06:56,205 --> 00:06:59,695
Gigapipe is an open source project,
a polyglot observability stack.

121
00:07:00,095 --> 00:07:04,065
This means logs, metrics, traces,
and profiles all ingested, stored,

122
00:07:04,105 --> 00:07:05,975
and queried from a single backend.

123
00:07:06,555 --> 00:07:09,575
Gigapipe is polyglot because
it speaks the languages of your

124
00:07:09,575 --> 00:07:14,155
existing tools, like for example,
Loki, Prometheus, Tempo, Periscope,

125
00:07:14,385 --> 00:07:16,695
OpenTelemetry, and many more natively.

126
00:07:17,125 --> 00:07:21,115
So the conclusion is that you don't
have to change your agents, you do

127
00:07:21,115 --> 00:07:22,610
not have to change your dashboards.

128
00:07:22,610 --> 00:07:27,165
You just point your telemetry exporter
towards Gigapipe, and that's it.

129
00:07:27,675 --> 00:07:30,255
Instead of four different
backends, now you have one.

130
00:07:30,955 --> 00:07:33,045
And Gigapipe runs as a single binary.

131
00:07:33,085 --> 00:07:37,905
It's open source under the AGPL v3
license, and it's used in production

132
00:07:37,905 --> 00:07:39,605
today by multiple companies.

133
00:07:41,001 --> 00:07:43,831
But because Gigapipe is fully
compatible with the industry

134
00:07:43,831 --> 00:07:47,311
standards, it's important to address
what it is and what it is not.

135
00:07:47,841 --> 00:07:51,961
Gigapipe is indeed compatible with
the biggest industry standards like

136
00:07:52,281 --> 00:07:57,221
Prometheus, Tempo, Loki, and this
means that your Prometheus remote write

137
00:07:57,481 --> 00:08:02,151
endpoint stays the same, your Loki push
API stays the same, your OTel Collector

138
00:08:02,191 --> 00:08:04,051
doesn't need a new specific processor.

139
00:08:04,131 --> 00:08:07,641
Data dog agents and InfluxDB
are also natively compatible.

140
00:08:08,171 --> 00:08:12,611
So this is important to retain, and
it is even more important to tell

141
00:08:12,611 --> 00:08:16,851
you that Gigapipe is a clear room
implementation of all of those APIs.

142
00:08:17,471 --> 00:08:19,071
Not a fork, not a wrapper.

143
00:08:19,341 --> 00:08:23,221
We wrote the code everything ourselves
from scratch, and that matters

144
00:08:23,311 --> 00:08:25,571
both legally and architecturally.

145
00:08:25,921 --> 00:08:28,631
So we are not a fork, we are not a proxy.

146
00:08:28,671 --> 00:08:31,121
There is no translation
layer on the hot path.

147
00:08:32,121 --> 00:08:36,651
Besides this, Gigapipe today uses
ClickHouse as its storage engine, and

148
00:08:36,651 --> 00:08:40,451
this was a deliberated choice that I will
explore further in a couple of slides.

149
00:08:41,121 --> 00:08:43,191
But the next version of
Gigapipe goes further.

150
00:08:43,551 --> 00:08:47,201
It will be fully self-contained
data lake native with Parquet files.

151
00:08:47,721 --> 00:08:50,291
No external database to manage at all.

152
00:08:50,291 --> 00:08:53,611
This is already in development, and
I'm telling you this because I want

153
00:08:53,611 --> 00:08:59,171
you to really take a note that Gigapipe
is a full potential solution for you

154
00:08:59,171 --> 00:09:03,351
today, but we are committed to become
a solution in the long run, so we

155
00:09:03,351 --> 00:09:07,921
are working towards the future way
of implementing and operating things.

156
00:09:09,351 --> 00:09:12,961
As I told you before, Gigapipe is a
single code binary you can run yourself

157
00:09:12,991 --> 00:09:16,831
on your infrastructure with your data
staying where you decide it to be.

158
00:09:17,311 --> 00:09:22,711
This means that Gigapipe doesn't impose
anything in your in, architecture.

159
00:09:23,011 --> 00:09:27,331
You can deploy it as you please inside
Kubernetes, VM, bare metal, you name it.

160
00:09:27,741 --> 00:09:30,751
If it can run a go binary,
it can run Gigapipe.

161
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:35,341
But furthermore, in terms of your data,
you are still the owner of it all.

162
00:09:35,481 --> 00:09:39,021
We do not make an imposition that you
need to send your data to a specific

163
00:09:39,021 --> 00:09:41,021
vendor or to a, the specific place.

164
00:09:41,351 --> 00:09:43,821
You own it and you decide where it lives.

165
00:09:43,941 --> 00:09:45,431
This is totally up to you.

166
00:09:46,371 --> 00:09:49,079
In terms of data visualization,
Gigapipe exposes a standard query API,

167
00:09:49,079 --> 00:09:52,931
small QL, PromQL, TraceQL, FrameQL.

168
00:09:53,301 --> 00:09:57,122
So this means that we are fully
compatible out of the box with Grafana

169
00:09:57,221 --> 00:10:02,051
and Persist, but also any UI that you
love that speaks those protocols can

170
00:10:02,051 --> 00:10:08,371
be used alongside Gigapipe And well,
now addressing the polyglot part of

171
00:10:08,371 --> 00:10:13,541
the thing, this means that we are
polyglot to read and polyglot to write.

172
00:10:13,661 --> 00:10:16,361
We have multiple opportunities
for you to explore.

173
00:10:16,831 --> 00:10:22,181
In terms of writing, you can push
natively OpenTelemetry OTLP protocol,

174
00:10:22,601 --> 00:10:27,221
the universal standard in the community,
already a CNCF graduated project.

175
00:10:27,641 --> 00:10:32,251
But besides of this, you can ingest
natively Loki, Prometheus, Stampa,

176
00:10:32,301 --> 00:10:36,001
Periscope, InfluxDB, Datadog, and Elastic.

177
00:10:36,101 --> 00:10:40,761
And these last three ones are worth taking
a pause because this means that if by

178
00:10:40,781 --> 00:10:45,431
any chance nowadays you are using one of
these proprietary vendors and you would

179
00:10:45,451 --> 00:10:49,921
like to explore with Gigapipe, you don't
h- don't have to make a huge commitment.

180
00:10:49,971 --> 00:10:52,861
You don't have to make a
decision before you know what's

181
00:10:52,861 --> 00:10:54,691
going on and if it suits you.

182
00:10:54,741 --> 00:10:58,901
Basically, you can deploy Gigapipe on
your development or staging environments,

183
00:10:59,301 --> 00:11:04,511
and you can point your Elastic or
Datadog agents towards it and compare,

184
00:11:04,531 --> 00:11:09,081
see the results, get to experiment
with the tool so you can get familiar

185
00:11:09,081 --> 00:11:11,501
without making this huge compromise.

186
00:11:11,951 --> 00:11:13,531
And this is in terms of writing.

187
00:11:14,051 --> 00:11:16,891
In terms of reading, as I told
you in the slide before, we are

188
00:11:16,891 --> 00:11:21,291
compatible with LogQL, PromQL,
TraceQL, FlameQL industry standards,

189
00:11:21,291 --> 00:11:24,891
but we also support InfluxDB and SQL.

190
00:11:25,771 --> 00:11:30,721
And all of these combined means that
everything flows through one single place.

191
00:11:30,761 --> 00:11:35,201
You have one backend, everything
lands in Gigapipe, and everything

192
00:11:35,211 --> 00:11:36,931
that you query goes through Gigapipe.

193
00:11:37,761 --> 00:11:42,791
So you have one unified time index
across all the signals, and this is why

194
00:11:42,811 --> 00:11:48,735
native correlation of signals becomes
possible So a couple of slides ago,

195
00:11:48,735 --> 00:11:52,445
I told you that we are using Gigapipe
and that it was a deliberated choice.

196
00:11:52,705 --> 00:11:53,355
Here's why.

197
00:11:53,735 --> 00:11:56,365
Observability data is very specific.

198
00:11:56,585 --> 00:11:59,295
Time series with high cardinality labels.

199
00:11:59,295 --> 00:12:02,845
You write constantly, you read in
time ranges, and you rarely update.

200
00:12:02,945 --> 00:12:05,665
That's exactly the workflow
ClickHouse was built for.

201
00:12:06,205 --> 00:12:10,205
ClickHouse is a columnar database,
which means that it only makes queries

202
00:12:10,205 --> 00:12:12,425
towards the columns that are needed.

203
00:12:12,845 --> 00:12:17,295
So for example, if you have a P99
latency dashboard and you make a query

204
00:12:17,305 --> 00:12:21,535
for the last 30 days, the columns that
the ClickHouse will read are only the

205
00:12:21,535 --> 00:12:23,675
duration and service name columns.

206
00:12:24,065 --> 00:12:25,485
Everything else is skipped.

207
00:12:25,695 --> 00:12:30,025
At billions of rows, that's the
difference between fast and unusable.

208
00:12:30,035 --> 00:12:32,845
In terms of reads and writes,
they are fully concurrent.

209
00:12:32,905 --> 00:12:37,255
This means that you can be ingesting
at full speed while making queries

210
00:12:37,315 --> 00:12:40,825
on historical data and neither of
these operations blocks the other.

211
00:12:41,805 --> 00:12:44,055
And ClickHouse is high cardinality safe.

212
00:12:44,415 --> 00:12:47,335
Prometheus breaks at high
cardinality because it holds every

213
00:12:47,335 --> 00:12:50,565
unique label combination as a
separate time series in memory.

214
00:12:50,575 --> 00:12:53,475
ClickHouse doesn't have a
time series model at all.

215
00:12:53,595 --> 00:12:55,095
Labels are just columns.

216
00:12:55,485 --> 00:12:59,145
A billion unique combination label
is a query performance question,

217
00:12:59,165 --> 00:13:00,565
not a storage architecture limit.

218
00:13:00,565 --> 00:13:06,505
In terms of cold data and hot data,
of course, with Gigapipe, we also

219
00:13:06,515 --> 00:13:11,365
offer that possibility, so all
telemetry tiers to S3 automatically.

220
00:13:11,745 --> 00:13:15,445
So it means that you can scale
horizontally and you are still

221
00:13:15,445 --> 00:13:19,495
the owner of deciding where
this S3 tiering happens towards.

222
00:13:19,815 --> 00:13:23,675
You can use ClickHouse Cloud,
you can self-host an S3 solution,

223
00:13:23,715 --> 00:13:26,935
or you can select any S3
compatible solution on the market.

224
00:13:27,495 --> 00:13:32,645
So you have one database, four signal
types inside of it, all your telemetry in.

225
00:13:33,275 --> 00:13:37,335
It works because the data shape of
each signal is actually more similar

226
00:13:37,335 --> 00:13:40,815
than the four different backends
that you are using would make you

227
00:13:40,815 --> 00:13:47,460
believe So, okay, let's take a look
at the architecture layers as a whole.

228
00:13:47,890 --> 00:13:51,720
We have an architecture that's
very simple, and each layer is

229
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:55,790
independent between each other,
and this independence is by design.

230
00:13:56,110 --> 00:13:58,250
So first layer is ingestion.

231
00:13:58,350 --> 00:14:01,800
Your agent's pointing data
towards Gigapipe and data flows.

232
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:05,800
No middleware, no transformation
overhead, nothing changes upstream.

233
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:08,630
Gigapipe is vendor-agnostic by design.

234
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:13,510
Then we have the storage layer that,
as I explained before, it's ClickHouse

235
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:17,950
for hot data, S3 compatible solutions
for cold data if you so desire.

236
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:22,320
Tiering happens automatically
by either TTL or space rules,

237
00:14:22,650 --> 00:14:24,330
and those are customizable.

238
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:27,490
You can decide whatever
works best for your use case.

239
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:30,790
Gigapipe gives you full
fidelity data retention.

240
00:14:30,790 --> 00:14:32,650
There is no downsampling needed.

241
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:37,160
Then we have the query layer, and
here we have this native support

242
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:41,920
for LogQL, PromQL, TraceQL,
FameQL, all implemented natively.

243
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:45,690
Because all the four signals share
the same backend, cross-signal

244
00:14:45,690 --> 00:14:47,969
correlation happens in a single query.

245
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:53,570
Finally, we have the UI layer, and we
are working out of the box with Grafana

246
00:14:53,619 --> 00:14:56,890
and Perses or our own built-in explorer.

247
00:14:56,930 --> 00:15:02,349
But as I told you before, any type of UI
that uses the query layer native protocols

248
00:15:02,349 --> 00:15:04,600
can be suitable to work with Gigapipe.

249
00:15:05,450 --> 00:15:10,050
And this is the reason why, one of
them at least, we made the layers being

250
00:15:10,050 --> 00:15:14,999
independently replaceable by design, so
you can adopt Gigapipe incrementally.

251
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,549
You can start with the storage
layer and keep the UI that you love,

252
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:21,939
or you can keep everything else
and just adopt the query layer.

253
00:15:22,289 --> 00:15:24,339
There is no big bang migration required.

254
00:15:24,380 --> 00:15:25,689
You can go in steps.

255
00:15:27,524 --> 00:15:31,004
But now that you know the source of
how Gigapipe works, the architecture,

256
00:15:31,034 --> 00:15:34,474
and the reasons for all the
decisions, it's important to address

257
00:15:34,484 --> 00:15:36,294
how fast is Gigapipe going to be.

258
00:15:36,294 --> 00:15:40,114
Because let's face it, you are going
to use Gigapipe and need it the most

259
00:15:40,194 --> 00:15:44,154
under pressure whenever you have an
outage, an incident in production.

260
00:15:44,724 --> 00:15:48,064
So to assess how fast it is,
we need to go layer by layer.

261
00:15:48,814 --> 00:15:51,704
Starting with the ingest, starting
with the ingest layer, ClickHouse

262
00:15:51,744 --> 00:15:53,344
prefers to sync inserts natively.

263
00:15:53,764 --> 00:15:57,324
There is no Kafka, no message
queue, no buffer layer to operate.

264
00:15:57,414 --> 00:16:00,963
Data arrives and lands directly in
ClickHouse's write-ahead buffer.

265
00:16:01,463 --> 00:16:04,284
Besides this, our native
protocol compatibility means zero

266
00:16:04,533 --> 00:16:06,383
transformation on the hot path.

267
00:16:06,974 --> 00:16:11,234
In terms of storage efficiency, ClickHouse
uses Merged Tree storage engine with the

268
00:16:11,283 --> 00:16:16,843
LZ4 compression, which basically means
we are under an awesome use case because

269
00:16:16,853 --> 00:16:19,353
observability data is highly repetitive.

270
00:16:19,783 --> 00:16:23,054
Timestamps, service names, status
codes, they repeat many times.

271
00:16:23,544 --> 00:16:27,314
And this columnar layout puts
identical values adjacent to each

272
00:16:27,314 --> 00:16:29,144
other and compression collapses them.

273
00:16:29,684 --> 00:16:33,633
So when you use Gigapipe, you can
expect a compression rate of seven

274
00:16:33,673 --> 00:16:36,683
times, ten times, and depending
on your use case, even more.

275
00:16:37,224 --> 00:16:41,764
So to give you a fair estimation, if
you ingest one terabyte of data, you

276
00:16:42,014 --> 00:16:46,284
only need one forty gigs of actual
storage to keep it, and this is

277
00:16:46,284 --> 00:16:50,893
consistent across all the signals,
logs, metrics, traces, and profiles.

278
00:16:51,834 --> 00:16:55,343
For the query performance question
columnar reads touch only the

279
00:16:55,343 --> 00:16:56,913
columns that the query needs, right?

280
00:16:56,973 --> 00:17:02,003
And ClickHouse processes one column
at a time in tight CPU loops, which

281
00:17:02,003 --> 00:17:05,983
means you can read billions of
values without context switching.

282
00:17:06,574 --> 00:17:11,893
And Gigapipe uses query fingerprint
indexing to reduce the search

283
00:17:11,893 --> 00:17:14,203
scope before any data is scanned.

284
00:17:14,593 --> 00:17:19,073
So these three things working together
is why you get fast queries on hot data.

285
00:17:19,573 --> 00:17:24,413
Working with a fast tool is crucial for
troubleshooting any type of incident, and

286
00:17:24,543 --> 00:17:29,913
it's even more important nowadays because
LLM integrations and MCP servers speak the

287
00:17:29,923 --> 00:17:32,223
